@clayjohn the Oculus desktop driver uses the same approach and from what I know of OpenXR it also will require this logic.

The way these systems work is that they require a texture chain to be created into which the images are rendered. So instead of using the render target Godot creates we provide the target and it changes for every eye and every frame we render. The SDK takes the chain of images to do timewarp logic as I understand it and keep the framerate up by reprojecting the images. 

You can always do a copy from what Godot renders to these texture chains (that is how I did the original Oculus plugin) but that is very wasteful and a definite bottleneck on mobile hardware so we came up with this approach.

